1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,300
The Causeway: A Kilometre
of Road That Runs Two Ways

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:07,834
The Johor–Singapore
Causeway has two rush hours,

3
00:00:07,914 --> 00:00:10,625
and they run in opposite directions.

4
00:00:10,625 --> 00:00:14,770
Before dawn, tens of thousands
of Malaysians who live in

5
00:00:14,850 --> 00:00:18,925
Johor Bahru pour south across
it to jobs in Singapore.

6
00:00:18,925 --> 00:00:22,994
On Friday nights and through the
weekend the current reverses,

7
00:00:23,074 --> 00:00:28,415
as Singaporeans head the other way — not
to work, but to fill their petrol tanks,

8
00:00:28,495 --> 00:00:31,360
buy groceries, see a
dentist and have lunch,

9
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:36,125
all at prices their own dollar buys
far more of on the Malaysian side.

10
00:00:36,125 --> 00:00:39,450
It is one kilometre of
road on a bank of rock,

11
00:00:39,530 --> 00:00:43,750
and the same currency gap is
worked from both ends of it.

12
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:47,130
For most of the 19th century,
everything that moved

13
00:00:47,210 --> 00:00:50,861
between Singapore and the
Malay Peninsula went by ferry

14
00:00:50,941 --> 00:00:54,592
across the narrow Johor Strait
— tin and rubber heading

15
00:00:54,672 --> 00:00:58,200
for Singapore's port, people
and goods heading back.

16
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:01,235
By 1911 the ferries were running around

17
00:01:01,315 --> 00:01:04,350
the clock and still could not keep up.

18
00:01:04,350 --> 00:01:08,248
The colonial authorities
wanted something permanent,

19
00:01:08,328 --> 00:01:12,379
and in 1917 they hired the
consulting engineers Coode,

20
00:01:12,459 --> 00:01:15,825
Fitzmaurice, Wilson &
Mitchell to design it.

21
00:01:15,825 --> 00:01:18,750
What they chose was not a bridge.

22
00:01:18,750 --> 00:01:22,944
Steel was expensive and a bridge
would cost too much to maintain,

23
00:01:23,024 --> 00:01:27,679
so the plan was a causeway: a solid
embankment of rock a kilometre long,

24
00:01:27,759 --> 00:01:30,638
carrying a railway, a
road, and the pipelines

25
00:01:30,718 --> 00:01:34,584
that would bring Johor water
to Singapore, with a small lock

26
00:01:34,664 --> 00:01:38,675
and a lift-bridge at the Johor
end so boats could still pass.

27
00:01:38,675 --> 00:01:43,801
The contract went to the London firm
Topham, Jones & Railton in 1919,

28
00:01:43,881 --> 00:01:45,838
and work began that August,

29
00:01:45,918 --> 00:01:52,024
quarrying granite from Pulau Ubin and Bukit
Timah and tipping it into the strait —

30
00:01:52,104 --> 00:01:55,650
around 1.6 million cubic
yards of stone in all.

31
00:01:55,650 --> 00:02:00,085
An economic slump in the early
1920s nearly killed the project;

32
00:02:00,165 --> 00:02:03,883
public criticism of the
cost, an estimated 17 million

33
00:02:03,962 --> 00:02:07,967
Straits dollars split between
the Federated Malay States,

34
00:02:08,047 --> 00:02:12,275
Johor and the Straits Settlements,
almost stopped the work.

35
00:02:12,275 --> 00:02:14,675
It was finished anyway.

36
00:02:14,675 --> 00:02:17,323
The strait was sealed by June 1923,

37
00:02:17,403 --> 00:02:22,545
goods trains crossed from September,
and on the 1st of October 1923

38
00:02:22,625 --> 00:02:26,600
the first passenger train
ran through to Singapore.

39
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:30,471
The formal opening followed
on the 28th of June 1924,

40
00:02:30,551 --> 00:02:34,870
in Johor Bahru, where a public
holiday was declared and the

41
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:39,045
Malay rulers and British
officials were driven across in

42
00:02:39,125 --> 00:02:43,300
a convoy of eleven cars before
the road was thrown open.

43
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:47,532
The Causeway had joined the island
physically to the peninsula,

44
00:02:47,612 --> 00:02:51,650
and it made Singapore the
terminus of the mainland railway.

45
00:02:51,650 --> 00:02:56,475
The Causeway's most dramatic
day came eighteen years later.

46
00:02:56,475 --> 00:03:00,548
As the Japanese advanced
down Malaya in January 1942,

47
00:03:00,628 --> 00:03:04,152
the retreating British
and Commonwealth forces

48
00:03:04,232 --> 00:03:08,150
fell back across the strait
onto Singapore island.

49
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:12,436
The last unit over, in the early
hours of the 31st of January,

50
00:03:12,516 --> 00:03:16,873
was the 2nd Battalion of the
Argyll and Sutherland Highlanders,

51
00:03:16,953 --> 00:03:18,844
marched across to two pipers

52
00:03:18,924 --> 00:03:22,375
playing "A Hundred Pipers"
and "Highland Laddie".

53
00:03:22,375 --> 00:03:25,768
Behind them, Royal Engineers
set off two charges:

54
00:03:25,848 --> 00:03:31,367
the first wrecked the lock's lift-bridge,
the second blew a gap about 21 metres

55
00:03:31,447 --> 00:03:35,700
wide in the Causeway itself and
severed the water pipelines.

56
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:38,475
It bought almost no time.

57
00:03:38,475 --> 00:03:41,551
The Japanese landed on
Singapore's north-west

58
00:03:41,631 --> 00:03:45,409
coast a week later, built a
girder bridge over the gap,

59
00:03:45,489 --> 00:03:48,575
and took the island on
the 15th of February.

60
00:03:48,575 --> 00:03:53,185
After the war the girder bridge was
replaced with Bailey-bridge sections,

61
00:03:53,265 --> 00:03:55,497
the lock and lift-bridge were sealed

62
00:03:55,577 --> 00:03:59,175
up for good, and the Causeway
carried on much as before.

63
00:03:59,175 --> 00:04:02,393
The pipelines the Royal Engineers cut in

64
00:04:02,473 --> 00:04:06,100
1942 are still there,
and they still matter.

65
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:09,694
Under water agreements
signed in 1961 and 1962,

66
00:04:09,774 --> 00:04:16,105
Singapore has the right to draw raw water
from the Johor River — up to 250 million

67
00:04:16,185 --> 00:04:21,186
gallons a day — and pipe it across
the Causeway to treat and use,

68
00:04:21,266 --> 00:04:25,800
selling a smaller quantity of
treated water back to Johor.

69
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:30,800
The 1962 agreement runs until 2061.

70
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:33,491
The prices in it have never changed:

71
00:04:33,571 --> 00:04:38,493
Singapore pays 3 Malaysian sen
per thousand gallons of raw water,

72
00:04:38,573 --> 00:04:41,575
and sells treated water back at 50 sen.

73
00:04:41,575 --> 00:04:44,644
Both figures were set
more than sixty years ago,

74
00:04:44,724 --> 00:04:48,384
and both governments regard
them as unfinished business —

75
00:04:48,464 --> 00:04:52,649
Malaysia has periodically called
the raw-water price far too low,

76
00:04:52,729 --> 00:04:55,536
Singapore has pointed
out that the agreement

77
00:04:55,616 --> 00:04:59,225
was a package neither side
reopened when it could have.

78
00:04:59,225 --> 00:05:02,050
Singapore now makes much of its own water,

79
00:05:02,130 --> 00:05:06,200
from recycling and desalination,
but Johor water still comes

80
00:05:06,280 --> 00:05:10,004
across that kilometre of
rock, and the arrangement is a

81
00:05:10,084 --> 00:05:13,750
reminder that the Causeway
carries more than traffic.

82
00:05:13,750 --> 00:05:18,519
On an ordinary weekday the Causeway
and the newer Second Link at

83
00:05:18,599 --> 00:05:22,837
Tuas together see something
over 300,000 crossings a day,

84
00:05:22,917 --> 00:05:28,443
which makes this the busiest land
border on earth; on the 6th of September

85
00:05:28,523 --> 00:05:33,675
2025 the two checkpoints recorded
543,000 crossings in a single day.

86
00:05:33,675 --> 00:05:36,823
A large share of that is
one specific movement:

87
00:05:36,903 --> 00:05:43,417
Malaysians who live in Johor Bahru, where housing
and daily costs are a fraction of Singapore's,

88
00:05:43,497 --> 00:05:46,725
and who work in Singapore,
where wages are not.

89
00:05:46,725 --> 00:05:48,775
The gap is stark.

90
00:05:48,775 --> 00:05:53,480
Average gross pay in Singapore runs
several times the Malaysian figure,

91
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:56,917
and for a Johor household
the difference compounds:

92
00:05:56,997 --> 00:06:00,017
a salary earned in
Singapore dollars and spent

93
00:06:00,097 --> 00:06:03,723
in ringgit stretches to a
house, a car and savings that

94
00:06:03,803 --> 00:06:07,375
the same job on the Malaysian
side would not support.

95
00:06:07,375 --> 00:06:10,808
So a few hundred thousand
people make the crossing a

96
00:06:10,888 --> 00:06:14,186
daily routine — up at four
or five in the morning,

97
00:06:14,266 --> 00:06:18,307
onto a motorbike or a bus,
through immigration on both sides,

98
00:06:18,387 --> 00:06:21,225
and back again twelve or more hours later.

99
00:06:21,225 --> 00:06:25,086
Whole parts of Singapore's
economy, from food courts and

100
00:06:25,166 --> 00:06:29,600
building sites to hospital support
staff, are staffed this way.

101
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:32,960
When the ringgit strengthened
by around six per cent

102
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:36,135
against the Singapore
dollar over the past year,

103
00:06:36,215 --> 00:06:40,434
it did more to narrow the wage
gap than any policy announcement —

104
00:06:40,514 --> 00:06:43,425
and Malaysian commuters
felt it immediately.

105
00:06:43,425 --> 00:06:47,150
The clearance itself
has actually got faster.

106
00:06:47,150 --> 00:06:50,360
For decades a crossing
meant handing a passport

107
00:06:50,440 --> 00:06:53,090
to an officer for a stamp — the "chop",

108
00:06:53,170 --> 00:06:56,600
inked with a "W" for
Woodlands or a "T" for Tuas.

109
00:06:56,600 --> 00:07:00,481
Now most travellers pass
through an automated gate that

110
00:07:00,561 --> 00:07:04,371
scans the passport, or a QR
code in the vehicle lanes,

111
00:07:04,451 --> 00:07:08,700
and takes a photo to match
against it — no officer, no ink.

112
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:13,432
Changi Airport has gone a step
further, to clearance on iris and

113
00:07:13,512 --> 00:07:18,545
face alone with no passport at all,
and that fuller version is being

114
00:07:18,625 --> 00:07:23,508
extended to the land checkpoints
in stages: facial recognition for

115
00:07:23,588 --> 00:07:28,550
motorcyclists from 2026, automated
in-vehicle clearance from 2027.

116
00:07:28,550 --> 00:07:31,293
What has not changed is the arithmetic:

117
00:07:31,373 --> 00:07:36,143
several hundred thousand people
trying to pass two sets of controls

118
00:07:36,223 --> 00:07:39,625
in the same few hours
each morning and evening.

119
00:07:39,625 --> 00:07:43,700
Automating the counter
does not widen the road.

120
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:47,012
Going the other way is
the second rush hour,

121
00:07:47,092 --> 00:07:50,175
and it is almost entirely discretionary.

122
00:07:50,175 --> 00:07:53,757
One Singapore dollar buys
roughly 3.3 ringgit —

123
00:07:53,837 --> 00:07:58,510
up from about 2.5 a decade ago
— and that exchange rate turns

124
00:07:58,590 --> 00:08:02,875
a routine errand into a saving
worth a morning's queue.

125
00:08:02,875 --> 00:08:06,509
Petrol is the classic reason
to go: Malaysian RON95 is

126
00:08:06,589 --> 00:08:11,254
price-controlled and costs well
under half what it does in Singapore,

127
00:08:11,334 --> 00:08:15,586
enough that Singapore requires
every departing local vehicle to

128
00:08:15,666 --> 00:08:18,956
leave with its tank at
least three-quarters full,

129
00:08:19,036 --> 00:08:24,125
and turns back the ones that don't.
Once across, the trip tends to expand.

130
00:08:24,125 --> 00:08:27,483
Groceries at the big
Johor hypermarkets run a

131
00:08:27,563 --> 00:08:30,925
third to a half cheaper
for the same brands.

132
00:08:30,925 --> 00:08:33,730
A haircut, a car service, a massage,

133
00:08:33,810 --> 00:08:36,775
new spectacles — all noticeably less.

134
00:08:36,775 --> 00:08:39,851
The food is the familiar
Malayan repertoire,

135
00:08:39,931 --> 00:08:43,875
cooked a little differently
and priced at Johor levels.

136
00:08:43,875 --> 00:08:48,376
And medical and dental work is the
category where the gap is widest:

137
00:08:48,456 --> 00:08:51,003
a scale-and-polish, a filling, a crown,

138
00:08:51,083 --> 00:08:56,797
a health screening in a Johor Bahru clinic
can cost a fraction of the Singapore price,

139
00:08:56,877 --> 00:08:59,828
and the clinics near
the checkpoint advertise

140
00:08:59,908 --> 00:09:03,950
their fees in Singapore dollars
to make the comparison easy.

141
00:09:03,950 --> 00:09:06,375
None of it is comfortable.

142
00:09:06,375 --> 00:09:10,398
A bad weekend queue at the
Causeway can run to two, three,

143
00:09:10,478 --> 00:09:15,925
four hours in a stationary bus or car,
in the heat, for the return leg alone.

144
00:09:15,925 --> 00:09:19,607
People do it anyway, because
the arithmetic still works

145
00:09:19,687 --> 00:09:23,175
out in their favour even
after the time is counted.

146
00:09:23,175 --> 00:09:26,252
For as long as the Causeway
has been congested,

147
00:09:26,332 --> 00:09:28,750
there have been plans to relieve it.

148
00:09:28,750 --> 00:09:32,668
A second crossing, the
Malaysia–Singapore Second Link

149
00:09:32,748 --> 00:09:37,345
between Tuas and Gelang Patah,
opened in 1998 and took some of

150
00:09:37,425 --> 00:09:41,650
the load without ever really
easing the Causeway itself.

151
00:09:41,650 --> 00:09:46,055
Malaysia has proposed replacing
the Causeway with a bridge more

152
00:09:46,135 --> 00:09:50,042
than once since 1996, most
seriously in the early 2000s;

153
00:09:50,122 --> 00:09:55,310
the idea foundered on the fact that
the Causeway cannot legally be altered

154
00:09:55,390 --> 00:09:59,875
without both governments agreeing,
and Singapore did not agree.

155
00:09:59,875 --> 00:10:02,675
The current fix is a train.

156
00:10:02,675 --> 00:10:06,287
The Johor Bahru–Singapore
Rapid Transit System,

157
00:10:06,367 --> 00:10:10,765
a short cross-border rail
shuttle between Woodlands North

158
00:10:10,845 --> 00:10:14,771
and Bukit Chagar, has been
under construction since

159
00:10:14,851 --> 00:10:18,700
2021 and is due to open
around the start of 2027.

160
00:10:18,700 --> 00:10:23,655
It is designed to move up to 10,000
passengers an hour in each direction

161
00:10:23,735 --> 00:10:28,200
and to cross in about five minutes,
and the hope is that it takes

162
00:10:28,280 --> 00:10:32,825
enough daily commuters off the
road to make a visible difference.

163
00:10:32,825 --> 00:10:37,451
A high-speed rail line to
Kuala Lumpur, agreed in 2016

164
00:10:37,531 --> 00:10:41,975
and cancelled in 2021, is
being talked about again.

165
00:10:41,975 --> 00:10:45,648
Singapore's Woodlands
checkpoint is being rebuilt

166
00:10:45,728 --> 00:10:49,175
to handle far more people
by the early 2030s.

167
00:10:49,175 --> 00:10:52,065
Between March 2020 and early 2022,

168
00:10:52,145 --> 00:10:56,600
none of this mattered,
because the border was shut.

169
00:10:56,600 --> 00:11:00,360
For close to two years the
daily crossing simply stopped:

170
00:11:00,440 --> 00:11:03,459
commuters had to choose
a side and stay on it,

171
00:11:03,539 --> 00:11:08,243
sometimes sleeping in Singapore for
months away from families in Johor,

172
00:11:08,323 --> 00:11:10,950
and the day-trippers had nowhere to go.

173
00:11:10,950 --> 00:11:15,350
The photograph at the top of
this post is from that period.

174
00:11:15,350 --> 00:11:18,498
It is the only time the Causeway

175
00:11:18,578 --> 00:11:21,200
has been empty since 1945.

176
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:25,475
The Causeway works because the
two ends of it are unequal.

177
00:11:25,475 --> 00:11:29,323
The wage on one side and the
prices on the other are what

178
00:11:29,403 --> 00:11:33,664
fill it every day, in both
directions, and every proposed fix —

179
00:11:33,744 --> 00:11:35,938
a second bridge, a shuttle train,

180
00:11:36,018 --> 00:11:40,623
a special economic zone — is really
an attempt to manage the traffic

181
00:11:40,703 --> 00:11:44,700
that inequality generates
rather than to remove the cause.

182
00:11:44,700 --> 00:11:49,186
A kilometre of rock laid down
in 1923 has become the place

183
00:11:49,266 --> 00:11:53,437
where the difference between
two economies is settled,

184
00:11:53,517 --> 00:11:57,925
one tank of petrol and one
dental appointment at a time.
